 from Marquette, TIPP, GBAO and RMG consistently put Crowley ahead by 3-5 points, and expert ratings lean Democratic, supporting a small but real Democratic edge in this open-seat race. Markets price Crowley much stronger than the polls alone, so the forecast lands slightly above the polling average, tempered by Tiffany's clear money advantage and a recent negative headline.", "watch": "The late-September drunk driving story and the next Marquette Law School poll could move the race either way before Election Day.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Tony Evers (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Wisconsin's governorship is open with Tony Evers retiring, and the race pits Milwaukee County executive David Crowley against Congressman Tom Tiffany. Polling through late August shows Crowley ahead by three to five points, including surveys from Republican-leaning sponsors, and most expert raters call the race Lean Democratic, with a few calling it a Tossup. Prediction markets are notably more confident, pricing Crowley's chances near 80 to 85 percent.\n\nTiffany has a large financial edge, raising over ten million dollars and outspending Crowley on air, which keeps the race competitive in a state with an even partisan lean. Both candidates are focused on affordability in the final stretch, and a late-September drunk driving story is roiling the race. The forecast gives Crowley a modest edge with meaningful uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7127, "margin": 2.83}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7543, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7549, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7725, "margin": 3.8}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7589, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7208, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.724, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7645, "margin": 3.5}]}